  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nRRFS still indicates fog potential up across north central South \nDakota later this morning, however there is a narrow ribbon of high \nclouds overhead. There also remains a slight gradient, helping to \nsustain a light wind (5-10 mph) with a south to southwest component. \nOnly Corson and Dewey counties have light winds currently. Will \nleave the mention of fog in for now, but it feels meteorologically \nlike kind of a reach. Dewpoints are in the 30s to near 40 so a humid \nairmass for sure.  \n\n\nThe gradient overall remains about the same today, though 1/2km \nwinds have fallen off slightly, with the RRFS mixed winds only into \nthe teens and low 20s. Compared to Sunday, 850mb temperatures are \nabout 2-3C warmer, so we should overachieve in comparison to Sundays' \nhighs.  \n\nThe gradient likewise remains about the same for Tuesday, though \n1/2km winds are higher, closer to 35-40kts (compared to 20-30kts \ntoday). Warmer air at 850mb also moves in ahead of a developing \nsurface low in Wyoming. The core of warmest air at 850mb is \novernight into early Tuesday morning, but deterministic models still \nshow 850mb temperatures topping out around 10-13C.   \n\nOverall trend is farther north with the low for Wednesday, between \nnorthwest South Dakota to well into North Dakota. This leaves us \nwith little if any moisture wrapping around into the northern tier \nof the state. The main impact of the shift in track is warmer and \nwindier for Wednesday. 1/2km winds in the warm sector remain around \n40kts, with 850mb temperatures of +9 to +13C. Dry frontal passage is \nshifted to the late afternoon/evening hours for the Missouri valley \nbut spreading rapidly eastwards between 00 and 03Z Thursday.  \n\nThe northwards track also means the core of coldest air is now only \ndown to around 0C at 850mb, though deterministic models vary by a \nfew degrees, the Canadian and RRFS still maintain above 0C with the \ncolder air remaining in North Dakota while the EC/GFS spread the \ncolder air behind the front across the CWA. \n\nFocus shifts to the next system, which is an upper trough crossing \nthe region, with a surface low across western Nebraska lifting \nnortheast across South Dakota late Friday/Saturday. Still a lot of \npotential range in outcomes regarding details. NBM highs Friday show \na wide spread in the 25th/75th percentiles range, from 13 to 17F. An \nexample of this is Murdo, with the lower range highs of low 40s to \nthe upper range being near 60F. That said, the track timing between \nthe EC/GFS are pretty close, and the Canadian is close in track \nalthough with a bit faster progression. NBM probability for Rain > \nSnow through Saturday morning but flips west to east through the \ncourse of the day. Not a lot in the deterministic accumulations \nthough.",
